              with a distinct 3D structure. We then observed the ants' return
              path in a test field on level ground. From the angles of the ants'
              return path on the test field one can infer which property of the
              hill segment was fed into the ants' path integration module, the
              actual walking distance or the ground distance. The results show
              clearly that it is the ground distance that Cataglyphis fortis
              feeds into its path integrator, and suggest that the ants are able
              to keep an accurate home vector also in hilly terrain.",
  year     =  2005,
  keywords = "Animals; Ants/*physiology; Desert Climate; Distance
              Perception/*physiology; Locomotion/*physiology;
