               Specifically, the concern is that social media algorithms
               combined with tendencies to interact with like-minded others both
               limits users’ exposure to diverse viewpoints and encourages the
               adoption of more extreme ideological positions. Yet empirical
               evidence about how social media shapes information consumption is
               inconclusive. We articulate how characteristics of platform
               algorithms and users’ online social networks may combine to shape
               user behavior. We bring greater conceptual clarity to this
               phenomenon by expanding beyond discussion of a binary presence or
               absence of echo chambers and filter bubbles to a richer set of
               outcomes incorporating changes in both diversity and slant of
               users’ information sources. Using a data set with over four years
               of web browsing history for a representative panel of nearly
               200,000 U.S. adults, we analyzed how individuals’ social media
               usage was associated with changes in the information sources they
               chose to consume. We find differentiated impacts on news
               consumption by platform. Increased use of Facebook was
