              and joint torques. Overall, we show that options enable temporally
              abstract knowledge and action to be included in the reinforcement
              learning framework in a natural and general way. In particular, we
              show that options may be used interchangeably with primitive
              actions in planning methods such as dynamic programming and in
              learning methods such as Q-learning. Formally, a set of options
              defined over an MDP constitutes a semi-Markov decision process
              (SMDP), and the theory of SMDPs provides the foundation for the
              theory of options. However, the most interesting issues concern
              the interplay between the underlying MDP and the SMDP and are thus
              beyond SMDP theory. We present results for three such cases: (1)
              we show that the results of planning with options can be used
