  author   = "Fiete, Ila and Schwab, David J and Tran, Ngoc M",
  abstract = "A Hopfield network is an auto-associative, distributive model of
              neural memory storage and retrieval. A form of error-correcting
              code, the Hopfield network can learn a set of patterns as stable
              points of the network dynamic, and retrieve them from noisy inputs
              -- thus Hopfield networks are their own decoders. Unlike in coding
              theory, where the information rate of a good code (in the Shannon
              sense) is finite but the cost of decoding does not play a role in
              the rate, the information rate of Hopfield networks trained with
              state-of-the-art learning algorithms is of the order $\log(n)/n$,
              a quantity that tends to zero asymptotically with $n$, the number
              of neurons in the network. For specially constructed networks, the
              best information rate currently achieved is of order $1/\sqrtn$.
