  pages    = "187--189",
  abstract = "In 1961, Rolf Landauer argued that the erasure of information is a
              dissipative process. A minimal quantity of heat, proportional to
              the thermal energy and called the Landauer bound, is necessarily
              produced when a classical bit of information is deleted. A direct
              consequence of this logically irreversible transformation is that
              the entropy of the environment increases by a finite amount.
              Despite its fundamental importance for information theory and
              computer science, the erasure principle has not been verified
              experimentally so far, the main obstacle being the difficulty of
