              issue using a model of a population of neurons that are broadly
              tuned to an angular variable in two dimensions. The fluctuations
              in the neuronal activities are modeled as Gaussian noises with
              pairwise correlations that decay exponentially with the difference
              between the preferred angles of the correlated cells. We assume
              that the system is broadly tuned, which means that both the
              correlation length and the width of the tuning curves of the mean
              responses span a substantial fraction of the entire system length.
              The performance of the system is measured by the Fisher
              information (FI), which bounds its estimation error. By
              calculating the FI in the limit of a large N, we show that
              positive correlations decrease the estimation capability of the
              network, relative to the uncorrelated population. The information
              capacity saturates to a finite value as the number of cells in the
              population grows. In contrast, negative correlations substantially
              increase the information capacity of the neuronal population.
