  pages    = "e25461",
  abstract = "Learned motor behaviors require descending forebrain control to be
              coordinated with midbrain and brainstem motor systems. In
              songbirds, such as the zebra finch, regular breathing is
              controlled by brainstem centers, but when the adult songbird
              begins to sing, its breathing becomes tightly coordinated with
