  number   =  3,
  pages    = "1099--1118",
  abstract = "Under normal physiological conditions, whole field visual motion
              generally occurs in response to either active or passive
              self-motion. In the laboratory, selective movement of the visual
              surround produces an optokinetic response (OKR) that acts
              primarily to support the vestibuloocular reflex (VOR). During
              visual world motion, however, the OKR can be viewed as operating
