              GABAA and glutamate receptor-mediated currents showed that these
              barrages of synaptic currents were predominantly of GABAergic
              origin. 5. The ENOs were totally blocked by the GABAA receptor
              antagonist bicuculline and they were also substantially reduced by
              the glutamatergic antagonists D,L-2-amino-5-phosphonovaleric acid
              (D, L-APV) and 6-cyano-7-nitroquinoxaline-2,3-dione (CNQX). 6.
              Synaptic stimulation and application of the GABAA receptor agonist
              muscimol mimicked the spontaneous Ca2+ transients in pyramidal
              neurones. The efficacy of muscimol in evoking Ca2+ transients
              decreased during development in parallel with the gradual
              disappearance of the ENOs. 7. The developmental decrease in the
              amplitude of ENO-associated Ca2+ transients occurred in parallel
              with the transformation of the excitatory synaptic transmission in
              the hippocampus from the immature GABAergic to the mature
              glutamatergic form. Thus, at the beginning of the first postnatal
              week single-shock synaptic stimulation produced Ca2+ transients
