              cellular mechanisms related to spiking. On a trial-by-trial basis,
              there was no correlation between number of spikes in the transient
              interval and the interval immediately after the transient period.
              This is opposite the prediction that there should be a correlation
              if spikes cause adaptation directly. Further, the transient was
              suppressed or extinguished, not delayed, in trials in which the
              neuron emitted zero spikes during the interval that showed a
              transient in average firing rate. We conclude that the transition
              from transient to sustained firing in neurons in area MT is caused
              by mechanisms that are neither input-specific nor controlled by
              the spiking of the adapting neuron. We propose that the short-term
              adaptation observed in area MT emerges from the intracortical
              circuit within MT.",
  month    =  jul,
  year     =  2002
}

