              activation. These rules apply to the control of both the agonist
              and antagonist muscles. Rules of activation lead to
              distinguishable patterns of EMG and torque development. All
              observable changes in movement kinematics are explained as
              deterministic consequences of these effects.",
  month    =  aug,
  year     =  1989
}

@ARTICLE{Gottlieb1990-fq,
  title    = "Organizing principles for single joint movements. {III}.
              Speed-insensitive strategy as a default",
  author   = "Gottlieb, G L and Corcos, D M and Agarwal, G C and Latash, M L",
  journal  = "J. Neurophysiol.",
  volume   =  63,
  number   =  3,
  pages    = "625--636",
  abstract = "1. Human subjects made discrete elbow flexions in a horizontal
              plane over different distances, from a stationary initial position
              to a visually defined stationary target 9 degrees wide. We
              measured joint angle, acceleration, and electromyograms (EMGs)
              from two agonist and two antagonist muscles. 2. Subjects made
