  volume   =  272,
  number   =  5258,
  pages    = "117--120",
  abstract = "For the last 20 years, it has been hypothesized that
              well-coordinated, multijoint movements are executed without
              complex computation by the brain, with the use of springlike
              muscle properties and peripheral neural feedback loops. However,
              it has been technically and conceptually difficult to examine this
              ``equilibrium-point control'' hypothesis directly in physiological
              or behavioral experiments. A high-performance manipulandum was
              developed and used here to measure human arm stiffness, the
              magnitude of which during multijoint movement is important for
