              movements was an 8-10 Hz periodic output to the muscular system,
              suggesting that slow finger movements are implemented by a series
              of biphasic force pulses, involving not only the shortening
              agonist muscle propelling the movement, but the antagonist muscle
              as well whose activity increased shortly after the agonist and
              contributed to a sharp deceleration of the individual step of
              movement. 7. It is proposed, as a hypothesis, that this biphasic
              motor output may reflect a similar organization of the descending
              motor command for slow finger movements. Hence, this command would
              include a series of biphasic pulses, concatenated at a rate of
              8-10 per second and a pulse-height regulator capable of setting
              the size of the pulse and thus the overall speed of the movement.",
