              strapped to the participant's back. Whole body kinematics were
              collected by placing infrared diodes on anatomical landmarks and
              using an Optotrak imaging system. Several measures related to head
              and limb movement were analyzed. Three major findings emerged from
              these studies. First, binocular vision is important for the
              acquisition of accurate information about the surrounding
              environment: accuracy but not precision of limb elevation over the
              obstacle was adversely affected when binocular vision was
              unavailable. Second, motion parallax due to self-motion provides
              the most critical depth information and it can be used to
              partially compensate for the loss of binocular vision. Although
