  volume   =  110,
  number   =  1,
  pages    = "96--108.e4",
  abstract = "To successfully perform goal-directed navigation, animals must
              know where they are and what they are doing-e.g., looking for
              water, bringing food back to the nest, or escaping from a
              predator. Hippocampal neurons code for these critical variables
              conjunctively, but little is known about how this ``where/what''
              code is formed or flexibly routed to other brain regions. To
              address these questions, we performed intracellular whole-cell
              recordings in mouse CA1 during a cued, two-choice virtual
              navigation task. We demonstrate that plateau potentials in CA1
              pyramidal neurons rapidly strengthen synaptic inputs carrying
              conjunctive information about position and choice.
              Plasticity-induced response fields were modulated by cues only in
              animals previously trained to collect rewards based on available
