              rat's velocity and heading direction. We consider the role of the
              network boundary in the integration performance of the network and
              show that both periodic and aperiodic networks are capable of
              accurate path integration, despite important differences in their
              attractor manifolds. We quantify the rate at which errors in the
              velocity integration accumulate as a function of network size and
              intrinsic noise within the network. With a plausible range of
              parameters and the inclusion of spike variability, our model
              networks can accurately integrate velocity inputs over a maximum
